    "---\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Data Understanding"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "437453bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2.1 Data Source\n",
    "\n",
    "This project uses data from the **2023 Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS)**, an annual health survey conducted by the **Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)** in collaboration with state and territorial health departments across the United States.\n",
    "\n",
    "The BRFSS is the world's largest continuously conducted health survey, collecting information on health conditions, health-related risk behaviors, preventive healthcare use, and access to healthcare among adults aged 18 years and older through telephone interviews.\n",
    "\n",
    "The 2023 survey contains **433,323 responses** and **350 variables**, making it one of the richest publicly available datasets for studying chronic disease risk factors.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Source:** Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). *Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS), 2023.*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "488d6abc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2.2 Why This Dataset?\n",
    "\n",
    "Type 2 Diabetes is influenced by multiple demographic, behavioral, and clinical factors rather than a single medical measurement. The BRFSS dataset captures many of these established risk factors, making it well suited for predictive modeling.\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset includes information on:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Demographic characteristics\n",
    "- Lifestyle and behavioral habits\n",
    "- Existing chronic conditions\n",
    "- Preventive healthcare practices\n",
    "- Physical activity\n",
    "- Body measurements\n",
    "- Socioeconomic indicators\n",
    "- Healthcare accessibility\n",
    "\n",
    "These variables allow the development of an interpretable machine learning model capable of identifying individuals at elevated risk of Type 2 Diabetes."
   ]
  },
  {
