    "## 3.6 Missing Value Imputation\n",
    "\n",
    "Following the recoding of BRFSS special codes, missing values remain across\n",
    "several variables in the working dataset. These missing values arise from two\n",
    "sources: structural `NaN` values inherited from the raw file where respondents\n",
    "did not provide height and weight measurements required for BMI calculation, and\n",
    "recoded non-informative survey responses such as *Don't Know*, *Not Sure*, and\n",
    "*Refused*.\n",
    "\n",
    "Missing values must be addressed before model training, as most machine learning\n",
    "algorithms cannot process `NaN` inputs directly. The imputation strategy applied\n",
    "in this section is **median imputation** for all features, selected for the\n",
    "following reasons:\n",
    "\n",
    "- All selected features are numeric and ordinal or continuous in nature.\n",
    "- Median imputation is robust to outliers, which are present in `_BMI5` and\n",
    "  `PHYSHLTH`.\n",
    "- For ordinal survey variables such as `GENHLTH`, `EDUCA`, and `INCOME3`,\n",
    "  the median preserves the ordinal structure of the response scale better than\n",
    "  mean imputation, which may produce non-integer values that do not correspond\n",
    "  to valid survey codes.\n",
    "- Median imputation is a widely accepted baseline strategy for tabular health\n",
    "  survey data and has been applied in comparable BRFSS-based prediction studies.\n",
    "  \n",
    "\n",
    "***NOTE***   Imputation here is for exploratory purposes only and the production imputation is embedded in the sklearn pipeline in Section 5\n"
   ]
  },
