    "The prevalence of diabetes increases progressively across successive age groups, demonstrating a strong positive association between advancing age and the likelihood of Type 2 diabetes. The lowest prevalence is observed among younger adults (18–24 years), while the highest prevalence occurs in the older age groups, particularly among respondents aged 65 years and above. This gradual upward trend is consistent with established epidemiological evidence, as prolonged exposure to metabolic risk factors, declining insulin sensitivity, and age‑related physiological changes contribute to an increased risk of developing diabetes. These findings suggest that age is one of the strongest demographic predictors of Type 2 diabetes and is likely to play an important role in the predictive models developed later in this study.\n",
    "\n",
    "Although diabetes prevalence increases steadily across most age groups, the trend levels off slightly in the oldest respondents (75–80 years). This may reflect survivor bias, differences in population size, or other demographic factors rather than a true reduction in diabetes risk."
