   "source": [
    "## 5.5 Handling Class Imbalance — SMOTE\n",
    "\n",
    "The target variable exhibits a **6.2:1 class imbalance**, with 86.07% of\n",
    "training records belonging to the negative class. Standard classifiers trained\n",
    "on imbalanced data tend to predict the majority class disproportionately,\n",
    "achieving high accuracy while failing to identify the diabetic patients the\n",
    "model is designed to flag.\n",
    "\n",
    "**SMOTE** (Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique) is applied to the\n",
    "**training set only** to address this imbalance. SMOTE generates synthetic\n",
    "samples for the minority class by interpolating between existing minority\n",
    "class observations, rather than simply duplicating them. This reduces the\n",
    "risk of overfitting associated with naive oversampling.\n",
    "\n",
    "SMOTE is applied **after** scaling to ensure synthetic samples are generated\n",
    "in the normalised feature space.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Important:** SMOTE is never applied to the test set. Applying it to the\n",
    "test set would artificially inflate minority class representation and produce\n",
    "misleading evaluation metrics."
   ]
  },
