   "source": [
    "**Observation — Retraining Without Class Weights**\n",
    "\n",
    "Removing class weight parameters produced different outcomes across the\n",
    "two model groups:\n",
    "\n",
    "**Logistic Regression and Random Forest** both models returned identical\n",
    "metrics to the initial training round, confirming that `class_weight=\n",
    "\"balanced\"` had no net effect when combined with SMOTE-resampled training\n",
    "data. The training set balance achieved by SMOTE was sufficient to guide\n",
    "both models to the same decision boundary regardless of the class weight\n",
    "setting.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Gradient Boosting Models** XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost also\n",
    "returned identical metrics, confirming that the low Recall in these models\n",
    "is not attributable to class weight parameters:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model | Recall (Initial) | Recall (Retuned) | Change |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Logistic Regression | 75.88% | 75.88% | 0.00% |\n",
    "| Random Forest | 65.38% | 65.38% | 0.00% |\n",
    "| XGBoost | 25.33% | 25.33% | 0.00% |\n",
    "| LightGBM | 20.77% | 20.77% | 0.00% |\n",
    "| CatBoost | 17.35% | 17.35% | 0.00% |\n",
    "\n",
    "This finding leads to two important conclusions:\n",
    "\n",
    "**First** the gradient boosting models' low Recall stems from their\n",
    "inherent tendency to optimise for overall accuracy on structured tabular\n",
    "data. With a 1:1 training set balance from SMOTE but a 6.2:1 imbalance\n",
    "at inference time, these models revert to majority-class predictions at\n",
    "the default 0.5 threshold. Their ROC-AUC scores (81.36%–82.00%) confirm\n",
    "strong ranking ability the problem is purely the decision threshold,\n",
    "not the model's discriminatory power.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Second**  SMOTE alone is insufficient to shift the decision boundary\n",
    "of gradient boosting models on this dataset at the default threshold.\n",
    "Decision threshold tuning in Section 6.8.2 is therefore the critical\n",
    "intervention for recovering Recall from all five models."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "06c6f3df",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 6.8.2 Decision Threshold Tuning\n",
    "\n",
    "All classifiers use a default decision threshold of **0.5** a patient is\n",
    "predicted as diabetic if the predicted probability exceeds 0.5. This threshold\n",
    "is not optimised for clinical screening, where maximising Recall is the\n",
    "priority.\n",
    "\n",
    "Decision threshold tuning is selected over GridSearchCV hyperparameter\n",
    "search for three reasons. \n",
    " 1. First, the ROC-AUC scores across all models\n",
    "(79.50%–82.00%) confirm that the models already have strong discriminatory\n",
    "power the problem is not the hyperparameters but where the decision\n",
    "boundary is drawn.\n",
    " 2. Second, GridSearchCV on a SMOTE-resampled training set\n",
    "of 343,000 rows with 5-fold cross-validation would require several hours\n",
    "of computation for each model, which is disproportionate given that the\n",
    "discriminatory ability is already established.\n",
    " 3. Third, threshold tuning\n",
    "provides direct clinical control by setting a minimum Recall floor of\n",
    "**75.88%** (the baseline), the optimal threshold is the one that maximises\n",
    "F1-Score above that floor, mapping directly to the clinical requirement of\n",
    "catching as many diabetic patients as possible.\n",
    "\n",
    "By lowering the threshold below 0.5, more patients are flagged as diabetic increasing Recall at the cost of Precision. The optimal threshold for\n",
    "each model is identified by plotting the Precision-Recall trade-off across\n",
    "all threshold values and selecting the point that maximises **F1-Score**\n",
    "while keeping Recall at or above **75.88%**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Threshold tuning is applied to all five models. A high ROC-AUC confirms\n",
    "each model has strong ranking ability and will respond well to threshold\n",
    "adjustment the tuning simply identifies the optimal operating point on\n",
    "the precision-recall curve for each one."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 70,
   "id": "0798c373",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "✅ Threshold tuning function defined\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.metrics import precision_recall_curve\n",
    "\n",
    "def tune_threshold(name, y_test, y_prob, min_recall=0.75):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Find the optimal classification threshold that maximises F1-Score\n",
    "    while maintaining Recall above min_recall.\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    precisions, recalls, thresholds = precision_recall_curve(y_test, y_prob)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # F1 at each threshold\n",
    "    f1_scores = (\n",
    "        2 * precisions[:-1] * recalls[:-1] /\n",
    "        (precisions[:-1] + recalls[:-1] + 1e-9)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Filter to thresholds where Recall >= min_recall\n",
    "    valid_mask  = recalls[:-1] >= min_recall\n",
    "    valid_f1    = f1_scores[valid_mask]\n",
    "    valid_thresh = thresholds[valid_mask]\n",
    "\n",
    "    if len(valid_f1) == 0:\n",
    "        print(f\"  ⚠️  No threshold achieves Recall ≥ {min_recall*100:.0f}%\")\n",
    "        return 0.5\n",
    "\n",
    "    best_idx    = valid_f1.argmax()\n",
    "    best_thresh = valid_thresh[best_idx]\n",
    "\n",
    "    print(f\"\\n  {name} — Threshold Tuning\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  {'─'*40}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  Optimal threshold : {best_thresh:.4f}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  Recall at optimal : {recalls[:-1][valid_mask][best_idx]*100:.2f}%\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  Precision at opt  : {precisions[:-1][valid_mask][best_idx]*100:.2f}%\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  F1 at optimal     : {valid_f1[best_idx]*100:.2f}%\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    return best_thresh\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"✅ Threshold tuning function defined\")"
   ]
  },
