   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6d026395",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **Observations — Global SHAP Feature Importance**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Rank | Feature | Mean Absolute SHAP Value | Interpretation |\n",
    "|:---:|:----------------------|:-----------------------:|:------------------------------------------------|\n",
    "| 1 | **Age** | 1.460 | Dominant predictor; age contributes most to the model's predictions. |\n",
    "| 2 | **Income Level** | 0.754 | Strong socioeconomic influence on diabetes risk. |\n",
    "| 3 | **General Health** | 0.555 | Self-reported health status is highly informative. |\n",
    "| 4 | **Poor Physical Health Days** | 0.452 | Greater numbers of unhealthy days increase model contribution. |\n",
    "| 5 | **BMI** | 0.392 | Obesity remains an important physiological predictor. |\n",
    "| 6 | **Hypertension** | 0.346 | High blood pressure provides substantial predictive value. |\n",
    "| 7 | **High Cholesterol** | 0.295 | Elevated cholesterol contributes meaningfully to predictions. |\n",
    "| 8 | **Education Level** | 0.213 | Educational attainment has moderate predictive influence. |\n",
    "| 9 | **Race/Ethnicity** | 0.203 | Reflects demographic differences captured by the model. |\n",
    "| 10 | **Sex** | 0.075 | Small but measurable contribution. |\n",
    "| 11 | **Kidney Disease** | 0.056 | Limited additional predictive information. |\n",
    "| 12 | **Smoking** | 0.050 | Relatively small contribution after accounting for other variables. |\n",
    "| 13 | **Physical Activity** | 0.045 | Low unique contribution to the model. |\n",
    "| 14 | **Heart Disease** | 0.039 | Smallest overall influence among all predictors. |\n",
    "\n",
    "The global SHAP feature importance analysis indicates that **Age** is by far the most influential predictor in the final model, with a mean absolute SHAP value of **1.460**, almost twice that of the second-ranked feature. This finding is consistent with the exploratory data analysis, where diabetes prevalence increased steadily across older age groups. The model therefore relies heavily on age when estimating an individual's probability of having Type 2 diabetes.\n",
    "\n",
    "Among the remaining predictors, **Income Level**, **General Health**, and **Poor Physical Health Days** emerge as the next most influential variables. Their relatively high SHAP values demonstrate that socioeconomic conditions and self-reported health status contribute substantially to the model's predictions. These findings reinforce the earlier EDA results, which showed higher diabetes prevalence among respondents with lower income and poorer perceived health, highlighting the importance of social determinants of health alongside traditional clinical risk factors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Clinical characteristics occupy the middle of the importance ranking. **BMI**, **Hypertension**, and **High Cholesterol** all exhibit moderate SHAP values, indicating that metabolic and cardiovascular health remain important contributors to prediction. Although these variables are well-established clinical risk factors for Type 2 diabetes, the model assigns greater overall importance to age and broader indicators of health and socioeconomic status.\n",
    "\n",
    "Demographic and lifestyle variables such as **Education Level**, **Race/Ethnicity**, and **Sex** provide additional predictive information but contribute less than the leading predictors. Education and race retain moderate importance, suggesting that they capture variation in diabetes risk not fully explained by other variables, whereas sex has only a relatively small effect on the model's predictions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Finally, **Kidney Disease**, **Smoking**, **Physical Activity**, and **Heart Disease** have the smallest mean absolute SHAP values. Their lower rankings do not imply that these factors are clinically unimportant; rather, they indicate that they provide relatively little unique predictive information after accounting for stronger predictors such as age, BMI, hypertension, cholesterol, and general health. In particular, the low importance of heart disease may reflect shared information with other correlated cardiovascular risk factors already included in the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the global SHAP analysis demonstrates that the model's predictions are driven primarily by **age, socioeconomic status, self-reported health, and metabolic health indicators**. The ranking closely aligns with established clinical knowledge and the patterns observed during exploratory data analysis, providing additional confidence that the model has learned meaningful and clinically plausible relationships rather than relying on spurious associations."
   ]
