   "source": [
    "## 8.5 Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "This project demonstrates that Type 2 diabetes risk can be predicted from\n",
    "routine self-reported health survey data with clinically meaningful accuracy,\n",
    "and that Explainable AI techniques can make those predictions transparent\n",
    "and actionable for healthcare professionals and patients alike.\n",
    "\n",
    "The final CatBoost model achieves a **Recall of 75.01%** and **ROC-AUC of\n",
    "82.00%** on a held-out test set of 85,818 respondents, correctly identifying\n",
    "approximately three in four diabetic individuals from 14 routine health\n",
    "survey variables without requiring any laboratory tests. SHAP and LIME\n",
    "explanations transform each prediction from a black-box output into a\n",
    "patient-specific risk profile that clinicians can interpret, verify, and\n",
    "communicate.\n",
    "\n",
    "The platform developed alongside the model combining a FastAPI inference\n",
    "backend with a Next.js dashboard supporting single and batch prediction,\n",
    "analytics, and PDF report generation demonstrates that machine learning\n",
    "models can be deployed as practical, user-friendly clinical decision-support\n",
    "tools rather than remaining confined to research notebooks.\n",
    "\n",
    "The findings support the growing body of evidence that machine learning,\n",
    "when applied responsibly and transparently, has significant potential to\n",
    "improve early detection of chronic diseases and reduce the burden of\n",
    "preventable illness at a population level."
   ]
